## ABSTRACT
Photodynamic therapy (PDT) is a widely-used technology for cancer therapy, but conventional photosensitizers still face some drawbacks, such as hydrophobicity, inadequate pharmacokinetics, low cell/tissue specificity, and uncontrollable photodynamic performance during the therapeutic process. Herein, we present a controllable photodynamic performance based on two-dimensional metal-organic frameworks (2D Zn-TCPP MOF) that displayed a weak PDT effect under a neutral environment upon exposure to a 660 nm laser due to the degeneracy of Q bands of TCPP. However, the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF showed a significantly enhanced PDT effect in an acidic environment under irradiation with a 660 nm laser due to the released TCPP from decomposed MOF structure. From the in vitro outcomes, the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF showed controllable photodynamic performance from neutral to acidic environments. Due to the acidic tumor microenvironment, the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF presented the strongest antitumor effect in vivo under irradiation with a 660 nm laser. This work offers a promising strategy to develop a next-generation photosensitizer.
## KEYWORDS
photodynamic therapy, metal-organic frameworks, tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin, acidic environment
## 1 Introduction
Minimally invasive photodynamic therapy (PDT) is a significant technological improvement that has been used as an alternative treatment to conventional radiotherapy, surgery, and chemotherapy [1–5]. Photodynamic treatment requires molecular oxygen, photosensitizer (PS), and lights to generate cytotoxic reactive oxygen species (ROS) to induce tumor cell death [6–9]. However, PDT treatment still faces some PS-related hindrances that mainly result from its hydrophobicity, inadequate pharmacokinetics, low cell/tissue specificity as well as poor stability within environment [10]. These all limit its clinical applications. An ideal clinical PS should have the following conditions: (i) not aggregated in the buffer, (ii) be chemically stable, (iii) low systemic toxicity to normal structure, and (iv) only be toxic under an appropriate wavelength.
Nanotechnology is one way to overcome the drawbacks of traditional PS and deliver the PS to a specified site and improve its therapeutic effect [11–15]. To date, several nano-sized carriers either purely organic or purely inorganic have been applied to PDT [16–21]. Organic and inorganic nanoparticles (NPs) such as polymeric nanoparticles, micelles, liposomes, and mesoporous silica have been investigated to encapsulate hydrophobic PSs via covalent or non-covalent interactions and overcome the solubility issues of PS and enhance accumulation of PS [22–25]. However, encapsulation of the PSs inside the
NPs induces PS self-quenching because of the excessively close proximity of PS molecules. In addition, the large amount of ROS in the NPs does not promote cytotoxicity because the average diffusion length of ROS is generally within 20–200 nm [26–27].
NPs induces PS self-quenching because of the excessively close proximity of PS molecules. In addition, the large amount of ROS in the NPs does not promote cytotoxicity because the average diffusion length of ROS is generally within 20–200 nm [26–27].  With this in mind, nanometric metal-organic framework (MOFs) NPs were built from the PS itself to benefit from the highest production of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). The rationale is that the PS ligand is highly ordered in the crystal frame, which prevents aggregation and self-quenching problems [28–32]. Lin's group reported various nano-scale MOFs applied for PDT such as DBC-UiO and DBP-UiO; these show very good performance for in vivo PDT [33–34]. In addition, a tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) based nano-MOFs has been widely investigated for PDT [35–38]. Upon exposure to a 660 nm laser, the resulting \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production from TCPP-MOFs was greatly enhanced relative to free TCPP. Despite this exciting progress, two obstacles still hamper the clinical application of TCPP-MOFs. First, \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production can also be generated in the healthy tissue under the irradiation of a 660 nm laser, inducing highly systemic toxicity. Zhou et al. tuned the ratios between TCPP and the switch molecule to control \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation via ultraviolet light, but the low penetration of ultraviolet light hinders the application of this controllable PDT effect [39]. Controllable PDT effect of TCPP-MOFs will be achieved if the attenuated PDT effect of TCPP-MOFs under an appropriate wavelength
can be enhanced via the tumor microenvironment (TME) like acidity pH or high cell/tissue specificity. Second, the TCPP molecule inside spherical TCPP- MOFs does slightly contribute to the PDT, but two-dimensional (2D) TCPP-MOFs are a promising structure to further promote PDT effect. Therefore, we envision that using TME-responsive 2D TCPP-MOFs can specifically control the PDT effect and improve cell/tissue specificity to reduce the systemic toxicity.
Recently, a researcher reported that the as-synthesized 2D Zn–TCPP MOF shows degeneracy into two weak Q bands at 603 and \(567~\mathrm{nm}\), while the free TCPP exhibits four weak Q bands at 645, 589, 547, and \(512~\mathrm{nm}\) [40]. In addition, the weak coordination bond of Zn–TCPP MOF between the hard Lewis base and soft Lewis acid causes their low stability [41]. Therefore, we speculate that decomposed 2D Zn–TCPP MOF can realize controllable PDT effects under the treatment of acidic microenvironment with irradiation of a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser. Herein, this work presents a novel strategy to realize controllable PDT effect based on the 2D Zn–TCPP MOF and acidic TME, improving the cell/tissue specificity. Scheme 1(a) shows that 2D Zn–TCPP MOF modified with polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) prepared via a one-pot strategy. The 2D Zn–TCPP MOF was stable under physiological conditions and showed almost no PDT effect under irradiation with a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser. The photodynamic effect of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF was enhanced with a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser after decomposition under the acidic conditions (Scheme 1(b)). The 2D Zn–TCPP MOF could efficiently generate \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under acidic conditions and induce cancer cell apoptosis. Thus, the 2D Zn–TCPP MOF could not only realize controllable PDT effects via the microenvironment but could also improve the cell/tissue specificity. In this way, such 2D MOF for this controllable PDT effect via the TME could be used as a next generation tool for specific cancer therapy.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals
The TCPP, zinc nitrate hexahydrate, N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), PVP (K30), triacetonamine (TEMP), and 9,10-anthracenediylbis-(methylene) dimalonic acid (ABDA) were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Corporation. Fetal bovine serum (FBS) and Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (DMEM) were bought from Gibco BRL (Eggenstein, Germany). Deionized
water was obtained from the Milli-Q System (Millipore, Bedford, MA, USA).
## 2.2 Synthesis of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF
To synthesize the 2D Zn–TCPP MOF, \(4~\mathrm{mg}\) of TCPP and \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) of PVP were dissolved in \(24~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF to obtain a homogeneous solution. Then, \(4~\mathrm{mL}\) of zinc nitrate aqueous solution \((1~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) were added into the above solution, and the mixture solution was reacted at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1~\mathrm{h}\) under stirring. After the reaction, the products were washed with ethanol and deionized water several times. Finally, the as-synthesized products were freeze-dried for further use.
## 2.3 Detection of intracellular ROS
For ROS observations on flow cytometry and fluorescence microscope, 4T1 cells were digested and resuspended into 24-well plates with about \(5\times10^{4}\) cells/well in \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMEM, and incubated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) atmosphere. Subsequently, \(100~\mathrm{mM}\) non-fluorescent \(2^{\prime},7^{\prime}\)-dichlorofluorescin diacetate (DCFH-DA) containing 2D Zn–TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\) and 5.0) or TCPP was applied to detect ROS, which could form the fluorescent matter DCF (green fluorescence) and could be measured with flow cytometry and a fluorescence microscope (Olympus, FV1200).
## 2.4 Animal and tumor model
Female Balb/c mice (5–8 week of age) were purchased from Vital River Laboratories (Beijing, China), and all animals were housed in compliance with the guidelines outlined in the Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals; all procedures were approved by the Guangdong Second Provincial General Hospital Animal Care and Use Committee. To obtain a xenograft model, the mice were injected subcutaneously with \(1\times10^{5}\) 4T1 cells (100 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\), \(10\%\) Matrigel) (BD Bioscience, Franklin Lakes, NJ).
## 2.5 Tumor suppression study and histology analysis
The Balb/c mice bearing 4T1 tumors \((\sim\,100\,\mathrm{\mm}^{3})\) were intravenously administrated with four groups \((n=5)\): (I) PBS, (II) 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \((15~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\), (III) TCPP \((10~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{kg}^{-1})+\) laser, and (IV) 2D Zn-TCPP MOF + laser. The (I) and (II) groups were used as control. The (III) and (IV) groups were treated with the \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser \((100~\mathrm{mW}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\). Subsequently, tumor volume and mouse weight were measured with the Vernier caliper and an electronic scale every two days, respectively. Finally, the tumor tissues were collected, weighed and pictured. The terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase-mediated dUTP nick-end labeling (TUNEL), Ki67-related antigen, and hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining of harvested tumor tissues were observed under a fluorescence microscope. The major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney) of each group were dissected to make paraffin sections for further H&E staining. The staining assays were performed on an optical microscope (Olympus, FV1200).
## 2.6 Statistical analysis
Statistical significance of the experimental data was assessed via the two-tailed Student's \(t\)-test, \({}^{\star}P<0.05\), \({}^{\star\star}P<0.01\), \({}^{\star\star\star}P<0.001\).
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF
The 2D Zn–TCPP MOF was fabricated through a simple
The formation of the 2D Zn–TCPP MOF was further determined by UV–vis absorption spectrum. The UV–vis spectrum of free TCPP showed four weak Q bands at 520, 557, 594, and \( 650\ \mathrm{nm} \) together with a strong Söret absorption at \( 418~\mathrm{nm} \) (Fig. 1(d)), which is due to the \( {\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+} \) transition between the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) [44]. After the formation of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF, the Söret absorption red shifts to \( 426~\mathrm{nm} \) , corresponding to the \( \mathrm{S}_{0}\rightarrow\mathrm{S}_{2} \) transition while four weak Q bands were degenerated in two weak Q bands at 564 and \( 605~\mathrm{nm} \) due to the \( \mathrm{S}_{0}\rightarrow\mathrm{S}_{2} \) transition. This conforms that the TCPP ligand was coordinated with zinc ions to form 2D Zn-TCPP MOF during the reaction process [45].
## 3.2 Detection of \( \mathbf{10}_{2} \) in solution
To investigate the ability of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF to generate \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) in neutral and acidic environment, the electron spin resonance (ESR) measurements were utilized to determine species of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) under \( 660~\mathrm{nm} \) laser irradiation. TEMP as typical spin-trapping agent was used to trap \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) in \( \mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) [46]. The ESR signal of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) was detected in a solution of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF in acidic environment with a 1:1:1 characteristic hyperfine splitting
constant \( (\mathrm{aN}\,=\,16.3\ \mathrm{G}) \) , which is a feature of the TEMP- \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) (TEMPO) spin adduct (Fig. S3 in the ESM). However, the \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) production efficiency 2D Zn–TCPP MOF in neutral condition was very low. Next, the ABDA was used as a sensitive probe to detect \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) because ABDA can capture \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) and produce endoperoxide that does not display absorption in the UV–vis spectrum [47]. The capture process was performed in \( 1~\mathrm{mL} \) of ABDA solution \( (0.5\,\mathrm{\mM}) \) with irradiation of deep-red \( 660~\mathrm{nm} \) laser \( (100\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m}^{-2}) \) for \( 5\,\textrm{m i n} \) . In the presence of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF \( (5~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}) \) in a neutral environment, the change in UV–vis absorption intensity of ABDA was not significant under laser irradiation (Fig. 2(a) and 2(c). However, the UV–vis absorption intensity decreased quickly in the acidic environment with \( \mathrm{pH} \) of 5.0 with the same irradiation time (Fig. 2(b) and 2(c)). In the control, the absorption intensity of ABDA was stable under the light irradiation at the same condition (Fig. S4 in the ESM). In addition, there is no significant difference in the ability to producing \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) by the free TCPP at different \( \mathrm{pH} \) values (Fig. S5 in the ESM).
We suspect that the enhanced PDT effect in the acidic environment is attributed to the released TCPP from the decomposition of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF that has a weak coordination bond of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF between the hard Lewis base and soft Lewis acid. To confirm our speculation, UV–vis spectra of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF before and after treatment with the acidic environment \( (\mathrm{pH}\,=\,5.0) \) were recorded in Fig. 2(d). In the spectrum of 2D Zn–TCPP MOF \( (\mathrm{pH}=5.0) \) , the peak at \( 656~\mathrm{nm} \) appeared due to the released TCPP in the acidic environment relative to neutral environment. Additionally, the liquid supernatant of acidic and neutral solutions at different time points was collected by centrifugation. Inductively coupled plasma-atomic mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) measurements were employed to quantify the released \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) ion. As shown in Fig. S6 in the ESM, the \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) ion had a burst release in the acidic solution within \( 30\,\mathrm{\min} \) while only \( 5\% \) \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) ion was released in neutral condition. The TEM image of decomposed 2D Zn–TCPP MOF was obtained after treatment with acidic condition confirming that the MOF structure was not stable in the acidic environment (Fig. S7 in the ESM).
These results demonstrate that the recovered PDT effect upon exposure to the \( 660~\mathrm{nm} \) laser is attributed to the release of TCPP under acidic condition. Therefore, 2D Zn–TCPP
MOF can be used as a novel PS for controllable photodynamic performance. Next, the cytotoxicity of 2D Zn-TCPP MOFs was evaluated according to the standard methyl thiazolyl tetrazolium (MTT) assay before biomedical applications. The 4T1 cancer cells were incubated with different concentrations of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF (ranging from 0 to \(100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) in the dark environment. The MTT results revealed that the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF exhibited low cytotoxicity against 4T1 cells indicating good biocompatibility of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF (Fig S8 in the ESM).
## 3.3 Detection of intracellular \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)
DCFH-DA can be oxidized to DCF (green fluorescence) by the ROS upon excitation at \(520~\mathrm{nm}\) [48]. The intracellular \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) levels in 4T1 cells after treatment with PBS, 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\), 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\), and TCPP \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\) upon exposure to a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser were assessed with flow cytometry analysis and fluorescence microscopy. The positive rate of green fluorescence in the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) group was approximately 3.1-fold higher than that in the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\) group (Fig. 3(a)), vividly indicating the enhanced PDT effect of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) group. The fluorescence microscopic images displayed obvious green fluorescence in the 4T1 cells after treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) and TCPP \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\) exposure with a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser \((100~\mathrm{mW}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 3(b)), which is consistent with the results of flow cytometry analysis. However, weak fluorescence intensity was observed after treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\). The mean fluorescence intensities of all groups were quantitatively analyzed from the corresponding fluorescence microscopic images, revealing a significant \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) level of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF
under the acidic condition (Fig. 3(c)). Although the green fluorescence of the free TCPP group was higher than that of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) group in the cellular level, biological application of free TCPP for PDT is limited because of its hydrophobicity and the low cell/tissue specificity of TCPP in vivo. Subsequently, the PDT effect against 4T1 cells was studied with the same concentrations of TCPP (ranging from 0 to \(5~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) in different groups including 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\), 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\), and TCPP \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\) upon exposure to \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser for \(5~\mathrm{min}\). The 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) treatment resulted in lower cell viability than that of the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=7.4)\) and control groups (Fig. 3(d)). By decomposing 2D Zn-TCPP MOF in the acidic condition, the released TCPP generated high \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) level upon exposure to a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser. The cell cytotoxicity was further measured by a live/dead kit. The results verified that the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(\mathrm{(pH}=5.0)\) group showed the most effective cancer cell killing ability (Fig. 3(e)), which agrees with the MTT results.
## 3.4 PDT effect of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF in vivo
To investigate the in vivo biocompatibility, healthy female Balb/c mice were injected intravenously with PBS and 2D Zn-TCPP MOF (20 and \(40~\mathrm{mg\cdot kg^{-1}}\)). The hematological parameters and blood biochemical levels were measured at two days post-injection. As shown in Fig. 4(a), the blood indexes of alanine transaminase (ALT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), white blood cell (WBC), and platelet (PLT) displayed no evident difference among three groups, indicating no significant toxicity of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF for mice during the evaluation period. Next, the pharmacokinetics of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF was measured with using 4T1 tumor-bearing mice. The blood
circulation half-life of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \((t_{1/2}=2.7\,\mathrm{h})\) was approximately 2.7 times longer than that of free TCPP \((t_{1/2}=1.0\,\mathrm{h})\) (Fig. 4(b)), which was attributed to the negative potential and nanoscale of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF. At 12, 24, and \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) postinjection of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF, all mice were sacrificed and their tumors and main tissues (heart, lung, liver, kidney, and spleen) were collected for bio-distribution analysis.
Owing to the clearance of the reticuloendothelial system, 2D Zn-TCPP MOF was primarily accumulated in the liver and spleen. Meanwhile, the concentrations of Zn-TCPP MOF in the tumor were \(4.8\%\) , \(6.4\%\) , and \(3.4\%\) at 12, 24, and \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) , respectively (Fig. S9 in the ESM). Therefore, the time-point at \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) after intravenous injection of samples was appropriate for in vivo experiments.
The in vivo ROS of tumors after injection with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF at different times was measured by staining stripped tumor tissues with DAPI and DCFH-DA. The 4T1 tumor-bearing mice were administrated with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF intravenously and irradiated with \(660\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) at 0, 12, 24, or \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) post-injection. Fluorescent images of the sliced tumor tissues showed an increased green fluorescence (DCF), and the highest fluorescence intensity was observed at \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) post-injection (Fig. 4(c)).
In vivo PDT performance of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF was evaluated against the 4T1 tumor-bearing female Balb/c mice. The mice were randomly divided into four groups \((n=5)\) when the volume of tumors was about \(100\,\mathrm{mm}^{3}\). Mice received an intravenous injection of PBS, 2D Zn-TCPP MOF, or TCPP. Next, the PBS, 2D Zn-TCPP MOF, and TCPP groups were irradiated with a \(660\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser \((100\,\mathrm{mW\cdot cm^{-2}})\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) post-injection. In the tumor growth profiles (Fig. 4(d)), the tumor size of PBS and 2D Zn-TCPP MOF without laser irradiation increased dramatically as controls. In contrast, the tumor size of the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser group gradually increased to \(\sim\,205\,\mathrm{mm}^{3}\) within 18 days. Tumor growth inhibition of TCPP \(^+\) laser group compared with PBS group was only \(\sim27\%\) due to the quick clearance of reticuloendothelial system. We observed the strongest antitumor effect with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser, which is attributed to the activated PDT effect from the tumor's acidic environment upon exposure to a \(660\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser. The body weight of mice showed no obvious changes during the treatment suggesting no significant toxicity (Fig. S10 in the ESM). The photos (Fig. S11 in the ESM) and weight measurements (Fig. 4(e)) of the tumors excised from mice on day 18 further verified that the 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser group was the most effective in suppressing tumor growth. For further evaluation the effect of photodynamic therapy based 2D Zn-TCPP MOF, the survival curves of tumor-bearing mice with various treatments were recorded (Fig. 4(f)). We found five out of ten mice after 30 days post treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser, in contrast to mice in the other three groups which all died within 25–30 days. These data emphasize the effect of in vivo tumor inhibition of 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser assisted by the tumor's acidic environment.
