Next, the collected tumors on day 18 were sliced and stained with H&E, Ki67 related antigen, and TUNEL. The histologic analysis showed that tumor cell density decreased significantly after treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser (Fig. S12 in the ESM). In addition, the Ki67 related antigen staining confirmed that treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF plus laser generated the most obvious damage to tumors among all the groups (Fig. S13 in the ESM). The TUNEL assay confirmed massive cell apoptosis in the tumor tissue after treatment with 2D Zn-TCPP MOF \(^+\) laser (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Histological examination of major organs including heart, lung, liver, kidney, and spleen showed no obvious pathological abnormalities
in the mice treated with various treatments confirming in vivo biosafety and biocompatibility of various treatments (Fig. S15 in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, 2D Zn-TCPP MOF was prepared as a photosensitizer via a one-pot strategy for controllable photodynamic performance. The 2D Zn-TCPP MOF showed weak PDT effect in the neutral environment upon exposure to a \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) laser, while enhanced photodynamic performance was achieved in the acidic environment because the decomposed MOF structure released TCPP under acidic conditions. The 2D Zn-TCPP MOF could not only realize controllable photodynamic performance but also reduce the systemic toxicity induced by ROS. This work may provide a promising strategy to develop a next-generation photosensitizer.
